1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
When the managed loop hides the details and you need to shape every turn yourself, you hit a wall.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The Agent SDK runs the loop for you, which is exactly the control that you sometimes need to take back.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This lesson covers dropping below that threshold and writing your own loop against the Messages API. Let's dive in.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Before we write any code, let's place the custom loop on a map.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
There are three levels of control you can pick from.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
At the bottom sits the Messages API, the raw model endpoint.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You call it, read the stop reason, run tools yourself, and re-invoke.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
That's the most control and the most code.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
In the middle is the Agent SDK, which is clod code packaged as a library.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
It runs the loop, executes tools, and manages context, so you configure it instead of writing it.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
At the top are managed agents, a fully hosted harness, where Anthropic runs the loop, the sandbox, and the sessions.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Today, we're living at the bottom level.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
So why give up the SDK's convenience?

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Because sometimes you need total ownership of each turn.

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
When you roll your own loop, you own tool execution, retries, and logging outright.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
There's no framework sitting between your code and the model, so there are fewer dependencies to track and pin.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Every request going out and every response coming back passes through code you wrote, which makes

18
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
deterministic behavior easy to enforce.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The tradeoff is real, though.

20
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
You're now writing and maintaining the plumbing the SDK gave you for free.

21
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
That's the deal you're signing up for, and for plenty of applications, it's absolutely worth it.

22
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Let's put the two side by side so the choice is clear.

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
With the Client SDK, meaning the raw Messages API, you write the tool loop yourself.

24
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:59,000
You call the API, inspect the stop reason, execute any tool use blocks, append a tool result, and re-invoke the model.

25
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
That's maximum control and maximum code.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
With the Agent SDK, Claude handles that loop for you.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
You configure the tools, the model, and the turn limits instead of writing the reason, act, observe, cycle by hand.

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Less control, far less code.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
The Client SDK is the right call when fine-grained control is the whole point of your build.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Here's the cycle every custom loop repeats.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
First, you send the messages array to the model.

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Second, you look at the reply and detect any tool use blocks in it.

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Third, you execute each tool your code owns and capture its result.

34
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Fourth, you append that result as a tool result block and re-invoke the model with the updated conversation.

35
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Then you do it again.

36
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
The loop keeps spinning until the model answers without asking for a tool.

37
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The one step people forget is the tool result.

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
If you skip it or you mismatch its ID, the very next call fails.

39
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Getting this handshake right is the whole job.

40
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
The loop runs on three blocks, so let's name them precisely.

41
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
First is tool use, a block in Claude's reply that names a tool and the input to call it with.

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
It's the model signaling that your code should act.

43
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Second is tool result, the block you append carrying the tool's output tied back to its tool use by

44
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
a matching ID before you re-invoke.

45
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Third is stop reason, the field you inspect on every response.

46
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
When its value is tool use, Claude has paused and is waiting for you to run a tool.

47
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Those three pieces are the entire contract between your loop and the model.

48
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Learn them and the rest is wiring.

49
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Let's zoom in on stop reason because your loop branches on it every single turn.

50
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
When the value comes back as tool use, that's your cue to run the requested tool and continue the conversation.

51
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,000
When it comes back as end turn, Claude is finished and your loop should exit and return the answer.

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Other values signal that you hit a cap or an error and your code decides what to do next.

53
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Here's the trap to avoid.

54
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
A stop reason of tool use means Claude paused and handed control to you.

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
It doesn't mean Anthropic ran anything.

56
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
In a custom loop, nothing executes unless your code executes it.

57
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
This is worth slowing down on because it decides how much work your loop does.

58
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Client tools run in your application.

59
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Claude returns a stop reason of tool use, and you're responsible for executing the tool and

60
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,000
returning a tool result.

61
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Server tools are different.

62
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Things like web search and code execution run on Anthropic's infrastructure.

63
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And their results come back to you directly, with no tool result needed from your side.

64
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
In a custom loop, your job is the client tools.

65
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
You execute them and feed the results back into the messages array.

66
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Knowing which side runs which tool tells you exactly where your code has to do the work.

67
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Let's be honest about when this is the right choice, because it isn't always.

68
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Not every application needs a hand-rolled loop, and reaching for one by default is a mistake.

69
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
But some builds genuinely benefit.

70
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
If you need deterministic policy applied at each step, a custom loop gives you that hook.

71
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
If you're integrating Claude into an existing runtime with its own conventions, owning the loop lets you fit right in.

72
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
And if you want minimal dependencies in production, fewer moving parts is a feature.

73
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The guidance is simple.

74
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Start with the simplest thing that works, and reach for a custom loop only when the control

75
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
genuinely pays off. Let's recap.

76
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
A custom loop hands you the raw model behind the agent SDK.

77
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
It matters because you control every turn, tool call, and logline yourself.

78
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Keep in mind, stop reason tool use is Claude pausing for your code to act, not Anthropic running the tool.

79
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
That covers building a custom agent loop.

80
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
When you're ready, I'll see you in the next one.

